  "ai_language_tip": "Begin in standard Malay (ms); most counter staff switch to English easily, and Chinese-run shops may prefer Mandarin.",
  "english_ok": "mostly — Schooling is bilingual, so offices, clinics, hotels and larger shops handle English; older stall keepers may lean on Malay.",
  "politeness": "Courteous and unhurried; use Awang (Mr) or Dayang (Ms) or plain Encik/Cik, keep a calm tone and close with terima kasih.",
  "name_tip": "Malay names are given name + bin/binti + father's name with no surname; book under the given name and repeat any Chinese or foreign name slowly.",
  "phone_tip": "All numbers are 7 digits with no area code; mobiles begin with 7 or 8 (712 3456), landlines with 2; read in a 3+4 split.",
  "time_tip": "Both 12-hour (pagi/petang/malam) and 24-hour appear; confirm 'malam 7' as 19:00 for dinner slots.",
  "number_tip": "Prices are in Brunei dollars (B$), pegged one-to-one with the Singapore dollar; say B$ before amounts.",
  "avoid_calling": "Avoid Friday 12:00–14:00, when shops pause for Friday prayers; during Ramadan many places shorten daytime hours and open late after buka puasa.",
  "closed_days": "The official weekend is Friday and Sunday; many private shops open Saturday; Hari Raya Aidilfitri closes businesses for several days.",
  "booking_channels": "WhatsApp is the default business chat; Instagram pages take orders; Dart handles ride-hailing; clinics at public hospitals book through the health ministry's BruHealth app.",
  "voice_menu": "Banks and telcos run Malay/English IVR; small businesses pick up themselves or answer on WhatsApp.",
  "callback_habits": "Shops reply by WhatsApp text or voice note; government clinics send appointment slips inside the app rather than calling.",
